sophia: Quick Q — we need a canonical pattern for mapping Redwood API errors to SDK exceptions across Python/TS. Goals: clear retry semantics, surfaced request IDs for support, good debug logs, and sane behavior for streaming partials. Example API response we see: `{status:400, error:{code: 'invalid_auth', message:'token expired'}, headers:{'x-request-id':'req_abc123'}}`. Thoughts?\n\nlee: +1. We should have explicit typed exceptions: AuthError, RateLimitError, BadRequestError, ConflictError, ServerError, NetworkError, StreamError, and a base APIError. Mapping idea:\n- 401/403 -> AuthError\n- 429 -> RateLimitError (retryable)\n- 408/5xx -> ServerError (retryable with backoff)\n- 400 -> BadRequestError (no retry)\n- 409 -> ConflictError (no retry unless idempotent op)\n- stream termination with error -> StreamError with partial payload and request_id\nAlso, always attach request_id and the original HTTP body to the exception for debugging.\n\nomar: Example Python sketch we've used internally, feels lightweight enough to copy into the SDK docs:```python
class APIError(Exception):
    def __init__(self, message, status=None, code=None, request_id=None, body=None):
        super().__init__(message)
        self.status = status
        self.code = code
        self.request_id = request_id
        self.body = body

class AuthError(APIError): pass
class RateLimitError(APIError): pass
class ServerError(APIError): pass
class BadRequestError(APIError): pass
class ConflictError(APIError): pass
class StreamError(APIError):
    def __init__(self, message, partial=None, **kwargs):
        super().__init__(message, **kwargs)
        self.partial = partial


def map_response(resp):
    rid = resp.headers.get('x-request-id') or resp.headers.get('X-Request-Id')
    body = resp.json() if resp.content else None
    code = body.get('error', {}).get('code') if body else None
    if resp.status_code in (401, 403):
        raise AuthError(body or resp.text, status=resp.status_code, code=code, request_id=rid, body=body)
    if resp.status_code == 429:
        raise RateLimitError(body or resp.text, status=429, code=code, request_id=rid, body=body)
    if 500 <= resp.status_code < 600:
        raise ServerError(body or resp.text, status=resp.status_code, code=code, request_id=rid, body=body)
    if resp.status_code == 400:
        raise BadRequestError(body or resp.text, status=400, code=code, request_id=rid, body=body)
    if resp.status_code == 409:
        raise ConflictError(body or resp.text, status=409, code=code, request_id=rid, body=body)
    return body
```\n\nnatalie: For retries, we usually do exponential backoff with decorrelated jitter and cap at ~60s. Also respect `Retry-After` when present. Important: do NOT retry non-idempotent POSTs by default unless client sets an idempotency key.\n\nryan: Streaming nuance: if the HTTP/2 stream dies mid-chunk, surface a StreamError that includes:\n- last successful chunk(s) (raw bytes or decoded messages)\n- the request_id header\n- whether the server reported a trailing error frame (if available)\nThat lets consumers either resume (if protocol supports resume) or surface partial output.\n\nomar: JS/TS example for attaching request_id and making it easy to inspect:```ts
export class APIError extends Error {
  status?: number
  code?: string
  requestId?: string
  body?: any
  constructor(msg: string, opts: {status?: number, code?: string, requestId?: string, body?: any} = {}){
    super(msg)
    this.status = opts.status
    this.code = opts.code
    this.requestId = opts.requestId
    this.body = opts.body
  }
}

function mapFetchResponse(res: Response) {
  const rid = res.headers.get('x-request-id') || undefined
  return res.json().then(body => {
    if (!res.ok) throw new APIError(body?.error?.message || res.statusText, {status: res.status, code: body?.error?.code, requestId: rid, body})
    return body
  })
}
```\n\nsophia: Nice. How should we expose debug logging in the SDK? We want logs that are safe to turn on in dev but not leak API keys or PII in prod.\n\nlee: Expose a `logger` hook and a `set_level('debug')` flag. Default logger redacts tokens/keys and truncates long inputs. Example log line: `DEBUG [request_id=req_abc123] POST /v1/generate status=503 retrying in=2.1s err_code=rate_limit` — include timing and token counts, no raw user inputs.\n\nnatalie: Also include correlation guidance: SDK should send `X-Client-Request-Id` (generated UUID) and attach server-returned `x-request-id` to all errors/logs. That way support can match client/server.\n\nryan: For other languages, same pattern: typed exceptions, request_id on the exception object, streaming error type with partial payload, and a retrier util that uses status + error.code heuristics. Keep logic centralized so new SDKs reuse the same mapping table.\n\nci-bot: deploy:shadow-sdk v0.14.1 published to the internal registry (includes APIError types and request-id passthrough).\n\nsophia: This is great — can someone open a small PR with the Python and TS snippets + a short policy doc (retry table + streaming behavior) and I’ll link it from the SDK README? Also put a sample log line examples section.\n\nnatalie: I’ll take the PR. Will include tests for: attaching request_id to exceptions, honoring Retry-After, and StreamError.partial includes last chunk.\n\nlee: FYI add integration test that verifies our SDK surfaces server x-request-id back to user on 500s. Saves SRE time.\n\nomar: I'll drop the python helper into `redwood_sdk/_errors.py` and add mapping tests.\n\nsophia: Perfect — thanks everyone :thumbsup:
